```javascript
function truncate(text, limit) {
  if (typeof text !== "string") throw new TypeError("text must be a string");
  if (!Number.isInteger(limit) || limit < 4) throw new RangeError("limit must be an integer of at least 4");
  if (text.length <= limit) return text;
  const room = limit - 3;
  for (let i = text.length - 1; i > 0; i--) {
    if (text[i] !== " ") continue;
    const head = text.slice(0, i).trimEnd();
    if (head.length <= room) return head + "...";
  }
  return text.slice(0, room) + "...";
}
```

Scanning spaces from the end finds the largest cut first, because the trimmed prefix only gets shorter as the cut moves left. With no space that fits, the first `limit - 3` characters are kept.

```javascript
function truncate(text, limit) {
  if (typeof text !== "string") throw new TypeError("text must be a string");
  if (!Number.isInteger(limit) || limit < 4) throw new RangeError("limit must be an integer >= 4");
  if (text.length <= limit) return text;
  const room = text.slice(0, limit - 3);
  const cut = room.lastIndexOf(" ");
  const head = cut === -1 ? room : room.slice(0, cut).trimEnd();
  return head + "...";
}
```

It takes the `limit - 3` characters that fit, backs up to the last space inside them so no word is cut, and appends the ellipsis. Without a space in that window the hard cut is used.

Here is `truncate`. It walks the text once, remembering how long the prefix is without trailing spaces at each space it passes:

```javascript
function truncate(text, limit) {
  if (typeof text !== "string") throw new TypeError(`expected a string, got ${typeof text}`);
  if (typeof limit !== "number" || !Number.isInteger(limit) || limit < 4) {
    throw new RangeError(`limit must be an integer of at least 4, got ${String(limit)}`);
  }
  if (text.length <= limit) return text;
  const room = limit - 3;
  let best = 0;
  let wordEnd = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < text.length; i++) {
    if (text[i] !== " ") {
      wordEnd = i + 1;
    } else if (wordEnd <= room) {
      best = wordEnd;
    } else {
      break;
    }
  }
  return (best > 0 ? text.slice(0, best) : text.slice(0, room)) + "...";
}
```

Since the text never starts with a space, `best` is only 0 when the first word itself is too long, which is exactly when the hard cut applies.

